    "description": "Multilingual communities in Iran bring together languages with different histories, geographic distributions, and public roles. Regional scholarship and research in Juneqan show why national language policy, local language contact, and individual linguistic identity need separate consideration. [s1][s2][s3][s4]",
    "tags": ["Multilingualism", "Language contact", "Community studies", "Language policy", "Regional identities"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:51:00Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:51:00Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Multilingual Communities in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran's multilingual communities through regional language diversity, constitutional provisions, and a local study of Turkic–Bakhtiari contact."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "languages-and-belonging",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Languages and belonging",
        "content": "Understanding multilingual Iran begins with a distinction between a country and a language family. In linguistics, an **Iranian language** belongs to a historical family descended from a reconstructed common ancestor. The term does not mean every language spoken by Iranian citizens. Persian belongs to this family, while other languages established within Iran have different genealogies. [s1][s2]\n\nGernot Windfuhr's regional overview identifies Turkic languages, Arabic, Neo-Aramaic, Armenian, Georgian, and Brahui among Iran's non-Iranian languages. These labels describe linguistic relationships, not degrees of national belonging. The same overview emphasizes that cultural, ethnic, and linguistic affiliations intersect: speaking a language does not by itself establish a person's entire identity. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-repertoires",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A regional mosaic",
        "content": "The Iranian language family itself contains considerable regional diversity. Prods Oktor Skjærvø's survey places Gilaki and Mazandarani along the Caspian region, Talyshi along its western shore, and several distinct language varieties across central Iran. These are parts of a historical linguistic landscape rather than interchangeable local versions of standard Persian. [s1]\n\nWindfuhr describes bilingualism and multilingualism as common in many regions, with Persian occupying a dominant public position and Azerbaijani Turkic also functioning as a language of commerce in some settings. This makes a map of single-language territories an incomplete guide to community life. A locality's language inventory and the languages an individual uses are different kinds of information. Neither should be treated automatically as an ethnic population count. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "policy-and-practice",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the constitutional text establishes",
        "content": "Article 15 of Iran's constitution designates Persian as the official language and script and requires its use in official documents, correspondence, texts, and textbooks. It also permits regional and tribal languages in the press and mass media and permits teaching their literature in schools alongside Persian. [s3]\n\nThe distinction between teaching literature in a language and using that language to teach the wider curriculum matters. Article 15's wording does not itself establish a general system of multilingual instruction across school subjects. Nor does the existence of a constitutional permission show how consistently it is implemented in particular schools. The legal text supports an account of formal provisions; evaluating educational access requires separate implementation evidence. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "juneqan-case",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Juneqan: contact beyond Persian",
        "content": "Research in Juneqan, a city in Chahar Mahal va Bakhtiari Province, offers a useful corrective to accounts that describe language contact only as Persian influencing other languages. Erik Anonby, Laurentia Schreiber, and Mortaza Taheri-Ardali examine contact between Bakhtiari and Qashqai Turkic through vocabulary, sound patterns, and grammatical structures. [s4]\n\nTheir analysis compares the first- and second-language speech of two bilingual individuals. The authors interpret the relatively balanced influence between the languages through the interaction of Bakhtiari's regional strength and the local predominance of Turkic mother-tongue speakers. This is a focused linguistic case, not a representative national survey. Its broader lesson is methodological: regional power, local population composition, and individual language experience can interact in ways that a simple majority–minority label misses. Understanding multilingual communities therefore requires attention to the particular place and relationship being studied. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
